{
  "about": "Each matrix row's chart: the table its mark reads (from tables) and the mark as the portable spec writes it, without its data; custom has no spec, so its proofs make it (packages/herceg/test/contracts/matrix.ts). Every table of rows has a field group of two values, N and S, which facets split by (the table of a network's nodes has none: facets split its links), and series and categories read the field kind (k and m) where the mark has them. constant marks a row whose mark reads no rows (an annotation at given values), which a chart of facets needs another mark beside to split. Histograms bin at given edges, so that facets bin alike. categories are the categories a row's legend lists, in order: what the mark tells apart by color; entry the one entry a mark of no categories lists for itself, where it lists one. colorbar gives a row colored by numbers: the options that color it so, and the extent of its colors, which a colorbar's labels stay within. toggle gives the options that list a row in the legend where it is not listed by default (annotations, with legend: true); hiddenStay marks a row that draws an entry's hidden shapes in its fill for no data rather than leaving them out (a map keeps its regions). Read by the matrix's proofs on every surface. selection says how the row's rows are selected (packages/herceg/test/browser/contracts/selection.test.ts): the binding's events where it takes only some, the gestures, in data values (fractional band indices on category axes; epoch ms on time axes), or the item an inspection names (label) and a square around its anchor, for marks without axes, the rows each gesture selects, chosen by the rules of src/model/selected-rows.ts, not read from the engine, and where a selection of the table's receive rows is drawn as is (on) and dimmed (off); selections gives each table's defaults: brushes' ranges, the point clicked, and the rows another view selects (receive). data names the table bound where the mark's rows are another's (a network's nodes); effect, the binding's effect. lifecycle says what moves the row (packages/herceg/test/browser/contracts/lifecycle.test.ts): the value its animation moves to, or that shows at once without one (change: field, row, value, a change the mark draws; lifecycle gives each table's), the axis or view its view moves along where it is not the first continuous axis (view: \"x\", \"y\", or a map's or network's view). reading says how the row reads (packages/herceg/test/browser/contracts/reading.test.ts): where the pointer goes (at, in data values, or an item by its label and the text an inspection gives it), the points it reads there as label, row (null for a summary), and text, by nearest (points), nearest-x (x), and nearest-y (y) where those differ, what nearest reads inside an item, nearer another's edge (inside), the tooltip fields a field of the table shows (fields, as label and text; field, fieldsAt, and fieldsMark give another field, target, or mark options where the row's own read no single row's fields), the first two announcements of the arrow keys (keys), and what the description names (describes); selections' read and field are each table's default target and field.",
